A: There's no way to automate the exact verification of contracts or any PEX test cases due to the inherent randomness and probabilistic nature of PEX. PEX will generate a multitude of test cases, and no automatic verification tool can verify each and every case.
However, here are some standard ways to review PEX test cases:


*

*Examine the PEX Test Explorer Output window during a PEX session for any failed test runs. This will likely give you a good understanding of what you might have missed.

*Analyze the code and manual test case to ensure there is a one to one correspondence between your test cases and code.


As for the contract assertions you mentioned, if you use the Microsoft Fakes Framework (Shim, Stub, and Mocking) you get the ability to validate your contracts. For instance, you can use a Shim to replace a method in your code under test and specify its behavior. You can then ensure your assertions hold before and after method invocation.
The Contract.Result would catch exceptions thrown by methods. It would only catch exceptions thrown if the thrown exception is equal to the result being returned.
Contract.OldValue and Contract.NewValue are typically used to examine parameters or local variables.
Contract.Ensures or Contract.Requires methods allow you to specify preconditions and postconditions. If these conditions do not hold in code under test, Contract.AssertFailed will be thrown in debug mode, and the corresponding contract will not hold. 
Contract.ForAll and Contract.Exists are used to state properties over collections.
All these features will help you to verify your pre and post conditions and invariants in your code.
Remember, PEX may generate tests that your Contracts do not cover, and Contracts may miss out some paths through your code. So cross-verification is crucial.

A: Pex produces a wide variety of tests, hence there is no one that fits all scenarios. The purpose of Pex tests is to cover as many cases as possible while providing valuable feedback about the tests that did not work as expected. If Pex cannot generate a test case you expect to be covered, then it's worth considering if you should add more preconditions or to manually write that test.
To examine the success of your tests, you may want to examine the results in the Pex Test Explorer. Failed tests typically provide good information about what part of your code Pex didn't get right.
Please note that your assertion about an exception caught by Contract.AssertFailed should be done like:
Assert.ThrowsException<System.ArgumentException>(() => yourFunctionUnderTest());

This will ensure that Contract.Ensures or Contract.Requires do throw the expected exception.
Lastly, Contracts in .NET are a good way to specify expectations on parameters, return values, fields and state of an object in a program before and after the invocation of a method. Use them to guard invariants or to ensure that function behavior is consistent with contract demands.
So, just to summarize, remember that unit tests and contract testing are just helpers and they should be viewed as complementary tools and not the encompassing truth. For some cases manual testing may still be required.
Q: Nginx 502 bad gateway I'm running an app (Gateway-598/1.4) which makes a lot of requests to a backend. I am using Nginx 1.14 and I'm experiencing a lot of "502 Bad Gateway" errors, which are causing a lot of trouble.
How could we potentially solve this problem? Are there any common causes of these errors typically?

A: The 502 Bad Gateway error code typically means Nginx could not get a response from the server it is trying to proxy to, possibly due to temporary server issues or overloading.
Here are some ways to potentially solve this issue:


*

*Ensure you have properly configured the upstream server in Nginx. The specified server should be reachable and running.

*Ensure your server is capable of handling the traffic. If you experience a surge in requests, it might be better to consider a load balancing solution that can distribute the traffic to multiple servers, or add more resources.

*Check the Nginx error logs to see if there are any clues as to what might be causing the problems.

--------------------        }
    }
}
