- This feature works well with .Net Core and .Net Framework projects.
- Pex-Sex and Pex-Code Contracts are excellent tools for testing .NET applications.
- Sometimes, it may need a little time to setup and configures the pex and itsex, or simply the settings in the project properties.
- In addition, It takes time in generating tests and may need for large scale projects as it may require a lot of time and resources.
- The more complex the application is, the more extensive are the test cases Pex may need to handle.
- It may also be difficult to handle complex data flow or control flow.
- Be aware that Pex may produce incorrect results in situations with high complexity, especially when using shared or uninitialized resources.

---

- You might also need to adjust the settings for the test code coverage report. The defaults might not align with typical usage and usage.
- In Visual Studio 2017, 2019, the feature was introduced with the `IntelliTrace` feature. It will capture state of application at the point of failure from Pex.
- The `Pex` can be very helpful in discovering the possible bugs in your code, but it doesn't replace a proper code review process or automated test cases. So, make sure to use it with other testing tools to get the most out of it.
- You may see a "x Unit Test" under Test Explorer in Visual Studio. They are created by Pex and are not executed by default. You need to manually add them to run the tests or use some automation frameworks to run them automatically.
- The results might not always be what you expect if the algorithm or logic of your code is complex as Pex is an exploratory testing tool.

Also, consider using a combination of these tools with others (Unit Tests, LINQPad, Sandcastle, etc) for a comprehensive approach to software development.

---

In summary, Pex is a powerful tool for finding bugs in your code. It automatically generates a set of tests that cover many scenarios, but like any automated tool, it has limitations and can occasionally miss edge cases. However, using Pex in conjunction with other testing methodologies can significantly improve the quality and coverage of your tests.

---
